hexagon: splat the scalar broadcast of REPEAT instead of one memcpy per element

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

The MTP draft head of patch draft-head/0001 builds the background of its scatter with
ggml_repeat_4d, which broadcasts one f32 into a vector of n_vocab. On the 4B that shape is
1:1 -> 248320:1, thus nr0 is 248320 and the destination holds one row.

The generic loop of repeat_job_per_thread calls memcpy once for each destination element,
with row_bytes of 4. Measured on the phone with GGML_HEXAGON_PROFILE=1, that op costs
**5.45 ms** to write 993280 bytes, which is 182 MB/s, about 280 times below the memory
bandwidth of this part. One destination row also leaves the row split with nothing to give
the other threads, thus it all runs on one thread.

The change has two parts:

- A typed store loop replaces the per-element memcpy when the source row holds one element
  and the destination row is contiguous. The compiler then uses HVX.
- A destination of one row splits along dimension 0, so that every thread works.

Measured after the change, 4B Q8_0 on HTP0, greedy, the same prompt, thermal 0 and off the
charger: the marginal cost of one draft position falls from 23.95 ms to 19.0 ms, and the
draft-3 speed goes from 15.81 to 17.23 t/s. The remaining gap to the byte model is the
SET_ROWS scatter at 1.5 ms.

Files:
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/repeat-ops.c

How to apply, from the llama.cpp root:

    git apply patches/hexagon-repeat-splat/0001-hexagon-htp-splat-the-scalar-broadcast-of-repeat.patch

